TI  - Cultural adaptation of nutritional recommendations in the comprehensive care of adults with type 2 diabetes: An updated transcultural diabetes nutrition algorithm (tDNA) framework.
AU  - Hegazi Refaat A
AU  - Hamdy Osama
AU  - Mechanick Jeffrey I
JO  - Reviews in endocrine & metabolic disorders
PY  - 2026
DO  - 10.1007/s11154-026-10099-6
AB  - The transcultural Diabetes Nutrition Algorithm (tDNA) applies medical nutrition therapy (MNT) for adults with type 2 diabetes (T2D) by aligning evidence-based dietary principles with cultural food patterns, clinical workflows, and health-system realities. To review the evidence underpinning the tDNA framework, describe its role in culturally adapting MNT across current treatment contexts, and identify priorities for research, education, and implementation. Sources include concept and framework papers, regional adaptations, randomized and pragmatic evaluations of tDNA-aligned programs, clinician toolkit assessments, and practice statements. Evidence was abstracted around tDNA decision nodes: risk stratification, carbohydrate quality and distribution, portion guidance, meal timing, physical activity prompts, use of diabetes-specific formulas (DSFs) as a meal replacement or supplement when indicated, and digitally enabled monitoring. Regional tDNA adaptations for Asian Indian, Malaysian, Canadian, Middle Eastern, Brazilian and Venezuelan settings are expert consensus documents specifying culturally appropriate modifications. A published randomized controlled trial reports that calorie-prescribed, glycemic index/load-guided tDNA integrating DSFs and augmented by structured behavior counseling improves glycemic control and body weight over 6 to 12 months, with gains in eating self-efficacy supporting adherence. We propose that MNT as a co-therapy be aligned to dosing when pharmacotherapy is initiated. For the highly effective glucagon-like peptide-1 receptor agonists (GLP-1 RAs) and sodium-glucose cotransporter-2 (SGLT2) inhibitors, culturally adapted standardized nutrition checkpoints are proposed to support nutritional adequacy. Whether structured nutrition therapy using DSFs preserves lean mass in this setting is an area for further research. The tDNA framework offers a structure for culturally adapted nutrition care in T2D. We propose its extension to current pharmacotherapy and digitally enabled delivery; these extensions require prospective evaluation. Recommendations include early localization, integration with drug titration, structured nutrition support where indicated, standardized nutrition checkpoints, digitized implementation, and audit of remission, time in range, weight, and adherence.
UR  - https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/42963492/
